## Safety boundaries

- A brief may declare an optional provider-neutral resource checkpoint in prose.
  At a safe boundary, finish the artifact atomically, record completed and
  remaining work, stop new work, and let the coordinator re-enter the ready
  gate. This adds no metadata key, profile field, renderer behavior, daemon,
  watcher, second CLI, or automatic resume, and does not assert that a resource
  signal has reset or that acceptance has passed.
- Do not rewrite Git history or bypass the publication gate with `merge`,
  `rebase`, `amend`, `reset`, force push, or tag. Source-only and offline
  evidence is not deployed or runtime-verified evidence.

## Artifact-first completion and handoff

- Write the required artifact to `${artifact_path}`. Prefer a temporary file in
  the same directory, validate it, and atomically rename it into place.
- Finish the artifact with the exact marker `${completion_marker}`.
- Before dispatch, the coordinator must record the resolved handoff transport
  and exact target in this task-specific brief. The rendered target is
  `${handoff_target}`. Do not probe alternative Herdr subcommands at completion
  time.
- Task-specific preflight note:
  ${notes}
- After the artifact and marker are complete, send exactly one applicable atomic
  handoff using the exact transport and target resolved
  by the coordinator before dispatch:
  `Task ${task_id}: artifact ${artifact_path}; marker ${completion_marker}.
  Please continue coordinator acceptance.`
- Do not poll for completion, inspect a transcript in a loop, run a background
  watcher, send a second Enter, or blindly resend the handoff. If the selected
  native or ordinary-pane atomic handoff fails, record that fact in the artifact
  and make no delivery claim.

